      "content": "Old medical theories often claimed that darker-skinned individuals had thicker skin or different nerve distributions, leading to higher pain thresholds and requiring higher doses of anesthesia.\nThese outdated justifications frequently relied on pseudoscientific observations of \"sluggish\" circulation in Black patients, which were used to argue they needed more aggressive treatment or had different physiological baselines.\nWhile modern science has debunked these racialized biological determinants, understanding this history is crucial so you don't accidentally apply these false premises to your current clinical reasoning.",
